@database "user_management"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: user_management.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Users"
@{h1}Users

When the server runs with sign-in, every person using it has an account:
a username, an e-mail address, a password, a role and an active state.
The @{b}Users@{ub} panel is where an admin keeps these accounts: creates
them, changes a role or a password, switches an account off, deletes it.
Only an active admin may open it, and the panel will not let an admin lock
themselves out -- so there is always one admin left who can fix things.
The plugin has no tools; agents never see it.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Users panel with four accounts"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Users panel  " link panel}
  @{"  Account rules  " link rules}
  @{"  Who may do what  " link security}

For developers
  @{"  The HTTP API  " link api}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Users panel"
@{h1}The Users panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Administration@{ub}, "Users"). The launcher
shows it to admins only. Anyone else is refused: by the route rule, or --
where that rule is loosened -- by the plugin, and the panel then shows the
server's refusal instead of a list.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Users panel"}

With sign-in switched off the panel says "Authentication is off" and
shows nothing else; see @{" Setting it up " link setup}.

@{h2}What you see

@{bullet}Three figures: all accounts, the active ones, and the active
admins.
@{bullet}A search box. It filters the table by username, e-mail and full
name as you type, ignoring case, and says when nothing matches.
@{bullet}The accounts, newest first: the username with the full name below
it, "(you)" on your own account and a key symbol on an account that has an
API key; the e-mail; the role; Active or Inactive (an inactive row is
dimmed); the day it was created and the day of its last sign-in, or
"Never". A click on a column head sorts by that column.

By default the list does not reload by itself. The reload button in the toolbar
loads it anew; the menu next to it turns on an automatic reload (every 30
seconds unless you pick another interval). When the list cannot be loaded
the panel shows the reason and hides the accounts it showed before, rather
than pretend they are still so.

@{h2}What you can do

@{table}
Button | Does
New user | opens the form for a new account
Edit (pencil) | opens the form with the account's e-mail, full name, role and state
Deactivate (ban) | asks, then switches the account off
Activate (check) | shown instead on an inactive account; switches it on again
Delete (bin) | asks, then deletes the account; it cannot be undone
@{body}

@{h2}The form

A new account needs a username, an e-mail address and a password; full
name, role (User unless you pick Admin or Guest) and Active are optional.
Editing, the username cannot be changed and the password field is empty:
leave it empty and the password stays, type one and it is replaced. Only
the fields you changed are sent -- a change someone else made meanwhile to
another field stays. When the server refuses (a name or address taken, a
password too short or too long, an address it does not take) the reason
appears in the form and nothing is saved; see @{" Account rules " link rules}.

@{h2}Your own account

You cannot deactivate, delete or demote yourself here: those buttons are
off on your row, and in the form role and Active are locked with a note
that another admin changes them. The server refuses the same, whatever a
client sends. You can change your own e-mail, full name and password. A
new password ends your other logins; the browser you set it from gets a
fresh login and stays signed in. If your password was changed elsewhere
since you signed in, nothing is saved and you are asked to sign in again.

@{h2}When the server refuses

A refused button -- the account is gone, you are no longer an admin --
shows the reason as a message, and the list is loaded anew.
@endnode

@node rules "Account rules"
@{h1}Account rules

@{table}
Field | Rule
username | 3 to 50 letters, digits, @{tt}_@{utt} or @{tt}-@{utt}; unique ignoring case; cannot be changed later
e-mail | a valid address with a domain; unique
full name | free text, optional
role | admin, user or guest
active | an inactive account cannot sign in
password | at least 8 characters and at most 72 bytes (UTF-8)
@{body}

@{bullet}The names @{tt}cli_user@{utt} and @{tt}anonymous@{utt} are
reserved, in any case: they are the identities of the command line and of
access without sign-in, and an account under either would share their
sessions.
@{bullet}Two addresses that differ only in the case of the part before the
@{tt}\@@{utt} count as two addresses.
@{bullet}The password is taken as typed: spaces count, nothing is trimmed.
The 72-byte limit is the one of the hashing (bcrypt); a password with many
accented letters or symbols reaches it before 72 characters.

@{h2}What a change does to the account

@{bullet}@{b}A new password@{ub} ends every login of that account made
before it -- browser sessions and tokens alike -- and removes its API key;
the account can make a new one after signing in.
@{bullet}@{b}A new role@{ub} holds from the account's next request: its
existing login is not signed again, the server reads the role from the
database each time.
@{bullet}@{b}Deactivating@{ub} ends the account's access at its next
request; activating gives it back with the same password.
@{bullet}@{b}Deleting@{ub} removes the account with its display
preferences and its count of password changes. A new account made later under the same
name does not inherit the old one's logins.

@{h2}Roles

@{tt}guest@{utt} is below @{tt}user@{utt}, @{tt}user@{utt} below
@{tt}admin@{utt}. What each may open is decided by the route rules in
@{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt} (@{tt}min_role@{utt} per path), not by
this plugin; it only sets the role.
@endnode

@node security "Who may do what"
@{h1}Who may do what

@{h2}Two locks

@{bullet}@{b}The route rule@{ub} in @{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt}:
@{tt}/plugins/user_management/*@{utt} needs a signed-in account with the
admin role. It also decides who sees the panel in the launcher.
@{bullet}@{b}The plugin itself@{ub} checks every request for the account
list or a change, whatever the route rules say: the caller must be signed
in with a login token (browser cookie or @{tt}Authorization: Bearer@{utt}),
and the account must exist, be active and be an admin @{i}in the
database@{ui}. The role written into a token counts for nothing; an API
key is refused; a refresh token is refused.

So a user or guest can list nothing, create nothing and change nothing
here, also when the route rules are loosened or the plugin runs under
another instance name. With sign-in switched off the plugin refuses every
request for data (403): there is no admin to check.

@{h2}What an admin may do

An active admin may list every account, create accounts with any role
(admin included), and change or delete any account but their own in these
ways:

@{table}
On their own account | Allowed
e-mail, full name | yes
password | yes; their other logins end, this browser's login is renewed (a cookie) -- a bearer token ends too
role other than admin | no (409)
deactivate | no (409)
delete | no (409)
@{body}

@{h2}The last admin

Every change is made by an active admin, and no admin can take the admin
role or the active state from themselves or delete themselves. So a change
through this panel always leaves at least one active admin: the one who
made it. Two admins demoting each other at the same moment cannot both
win either -- the plugin checks the caller against the database right
before it writes, with nothing in between in which the other request
could run.

That holds for the requests one server handles. The command line
(@{tt}agent-cli users ...@{utt}) writes the database directly and is not
checked, and neither is a second server on the same database. If no
active admin is left, the command line is the way back:

@{code bash}
agent-cli users update <name> --role admin --activate
@{body}

@{h2}What never leaves the server

No answer carries a password hash or an API key; the list says only
whether an account has a key. Fields a request sends that the plugin does
not take -- a password hash, an API key, a username in a change -- are
ignored. A creation must come as JSON: a plain form post, which another
web page could send in your name, is refused.
@endnode

@node api "The HTTP API"
@{h1}The HTTP API

Everything under @{tt}/plugins/user_management/@{utt} (the plugin's
instance name), JSON in and out. The panel uses exactly these; every data
route checks the caller as in @{" Who may do what " link security}.

@{table}
Method | Path | Does
GET | / | the panel page
GET | /users | @{tt}{"me": <your id>, "users": [...]}@{utt}: every account, newest first
POST | /users | creates an account: username, email, password; full_name, role, is_active optional
PUT | /users/{id} | changes any of email, full_name, role, is_active, password; only the fields sent
DELETE | /users/{id} | deletes the account; answers @{tt}{"deleted": <id>}@{utt}
@{body}

Each account in an answer has @{tt}id@{utt}, @{tt}username@{utt},
@{tt}email@{utt}, @{tt}full_name@{utt}, @{tt}role@{utt},
@{tt}is_active@{utt}, @{tt}created_at@{utt}, @{tt}last_login@{utt} and
@{tt}has_api_key@{utt}. POST and PUT answer with the account as it is
now. A PUT on your own account with a new password also sets a fresh
@{tt}access_token@{utt} cookie.

@{h2}Refusals

@{table}
Status | When
401 | not signed in, an expired or refresh token, an API key, an account deleted since
403 | not an active admin, or sign-in switched off
404 | no account with that id
409 | username or e-mail taken, a reserved username, a change to your own role, active state or existence, your own password changed meanwhile
415 | a creation sent without @{tt}Content-Type: application/json@{utt}
422 | a field against the @{" account rules " link rules}, an unknown role
@{body}

The refusal's @{tt}detail@{utt} says why, for example "Admin privileges
required", "Username 'alice' already exists" or "You cannot take your own
admin role or deactivate yourself"; a 422 lists each field that failed.

@{h2}An example

@{code bash}
curl -X PUT http://<server>/plugins/user_management/users/7 \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"role": "guest"}'
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    user_management:
      type: user_management
      enabled: true
@{body}

It has no settings of its own and needs no package beyond the server's.
What it works on is the server's sign-in, in @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}:

@{table}
Key | Default in code | Meaning
auth.enabled | false | true: accounts and sign-in; false: the panel says so and the API refuses
auth.database_path | data/users.db | the accounts database; a data/... path lands in the data directory
@{body}

The first admin comes from the command line, on the machine the server
runs on:

@{code bash}
agent-cli users create <name> <email> --admin
@{body}

It asks for the password. From then on that admin creates the other
accounts in the panel.

The route rule that keeps the panel for admins is in
@{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt}, under
@{tt}auth.plugin_security.endpoint_rules@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
- pattern: "/plugins/user_management/*"
  policy: "require_auth"
  min_role: "admin"
@{body}

The plugin checks for an admin itself as well, so the rule is not what
keeps the accounts safe -- it keeps the panel out of other people's
launchers.
@endnode
